Two objects referencing each other, with nothing else pointing to either, are still collected — mark-and-sweep doesn't get fooled by cycles the way naive reference counting would.

```javascript
function makePair() {
  const a = {};
  const b = {};
  a.next = b;
  b.next = a; // a cycle: a points to b, b points to a
  return null; // nothing outside this function references a or b anymore
}
makePair();
```

`a` and `b` reference each other, but neither is reachable from any root once `makePair` returns — the mark phase never reaches them starting from the roots, so both get swept despite the cycle. (Older reference-counting collectors, which some other languages use, would leak exactly this pattern, since each object's count never drops to zero.)

