A regular `Map` used as a cache keyed by DOM elements or objects **prevents** those keys from ever being garbage collected, because the `Map` itself is a strong reference — as long as the `Map` is alive, so is every key inside it, even if nothing else in the program references that key anymore.

```javascript
const cache = new Map();       // strong reference — leaks if elements are never explicitly deleted
const weakCache = new WeakMap(); // weak reference — doesn't prevent garbage collection

function attachMetadata(el, data) {
  weakCache.set(el, data);
}
// once el has no other references and is removed from the DOM,
// its entry in weakCache is automatically eligible for collection too
```

`WeakMap`/`WeakSet` hold their keys **weakly** — a key's presence in a `WeakMap` never counts toward keeping it reachable. This is exactly the tool for "attach extra data to an object without extending its lifetime," and it's why `WeakMap` keys must be objects, not primitives — primitives don't have a garbage-collectible identity to hook a weak reference to.

```javascript
class Widget {
  constructor(el) {
    this.el = el;
    this.onResize = () => this.render(); // closes over `this`
    window.addEventListener("resize", this.onResize);
  }
  render() { /* ... */ }
  destroy() {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", this.onResize); // the missing piece
    this.el = null;
  }
}
```

Without `destroy()` calling `removeEventListener`, `window` — which lives for the entire page lifetime — holds a reference to `this.onResize`, which closes over the entire `Widget` instance via `this`. Every `Widget` ever created and "removed" from the page stays alive in memory, still reachable through `window`'s listener list, growing without bound as the user navigates.

The fix is symmetric and mandatory: whatever lifecycle removes the widget from the page must also call the matching `removeEventListener`, passing the **exact same function reference** that was added — a structurally identical but separately-created arrow function will not match and silently fails to remove anything.

Revisiting the [closures](closures) memory-leak pattern with the reachability model now in hand: a closure keeps its **entire enclosing variable environment** reachable, not just the specific variables it references — so a small, long-lived function can pin a surprisingly large amount of unrelated memory if it happens to share a scope with something big.

```javascript
function setup() {
  const hugeBuffer = new ArrayBuffer(50_000_000); // ~50MB
  const tinyHandler = () => console.log("clicked");
  document.addEventListener("click", tinyHandler);
}
```

`tinyHandler` never touches `hugeBuffer`, but depending on how the engine's scope analysis works, both can end up in the same variable environment object — and `document`'s listener list holds `tinyHandler` alive for the page's lifetime, potentially keeping `hugeBuffer` reachable the whole time too. The practical defense: don't let a long-lived callback share a function scope with large, short-lived data — declare them in genuinely separate scopes, or null out large references once you're done with them.
